    <string name="tpl_tone_title">This may sound promotional</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_explainer">Your sender ID is registered as transactional. Safaricom can block messages that sound like an advert instead of a receipt.</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_issue">%1$s — %2$s</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_urgency">Urgency language like this reads as marketing copy, not a receipt. State what happened, not why they should hurry.</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_cta">This pushes toward a new purchase, which reads as an advert. Say what already happened instead.</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_discount">Discount or sale language is a strong promotional signal. State a price plainly if you need to, without the sale framing.</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_prize">Prize or giveaway language reads as a marketing hook, not a transaction record.</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_shouting">Writing in ALL CAPS reads as shouting, an advertising tell. Use normal sentence case.</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_punctuation">Repeated \"!\" or \"?\" reads as promotional excitement. One is plenty, or none.</string>
    <string name="tpl_tone_emoji">Several emoji read as casual/promotional rather than a transaction notice — and each one risks doubling this message\'s SMS cost.</string>

